WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.000
[MUSIC]

00:00:18.973 --> 00:00:23.070
Offline compared to online retail is still
actually a very large faction of consumer

00:00:23.070 --> 00:00:23.655
spending.

00:00:23.655 --> 00:00:28.317
So, although online is growing quite
dramatically, as of now essentially still

00:00:28.317 --> 00:00:32.340
about 90% of retail spending
happens in the offline world.

00:00:32.340 --> 00:00:36.390
And firms are still trying to figure
out how to best promote products,

00:00:36.390 --> 00:00:40.140
how to best run a price promotions,
things of that sort.

00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:44.039
And so, we wanted to go back and
see, can we actually use tools

00:00:45.070 --> 00:00:47.960
that we learned about in the online world,
better types of measurement.

00:00:47.960 --> 00:00:51.616
And can we bring that back to the offline
world, and try to track things better?

00:00:51.616 --> 00:00:56.207
[MUSIC]

00:00:56.207 --> 00:00:59.044
So we know if I run an ad online,
I typically can track,

00:00:59.044 --> 00:01:03.780
did somebody click on it, did they again
go to my website, how much they spend.

00:01:03.780 --> 00:01:05.770
All this linkage is much closer, and

00:01:05.770 --> 00:01:08.420
those are often things that we
don't observe in the offline world.

00:01:08.420 --> 00:01:10.750
Right, so an online retailer would know,

00:01:10.750 --> 00:01:13.450
how did this consumer get to
the product he ultimately purchased?

00:01:13.450 --> 00:01:15.695
We can observe their browsing
with a lot of precision,

00:01:15.695 --> 00:01:17.650
offline there's usually no equivalent.

00:01:17.650 --> 00:01:21.290
Traditionally, we weren't able to see
how did somebody walk through the store?

00:01:21.290 --> 00:01:25.010
How did they actually get to the specific
aisle, where they then picked up products?

00:01:25.010 --> 00:01:25.920
And so,

00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:29.780
what we do in the study is we actually
bring in a new measurement tool that's

00:01:29.780 --> 00:01:33.630
going to mimic the way we can observe
things online, in the offline world.

00:01:33.630 --> 00:01:36.160
And in particular what we
going to do is we're going to rig

00:01:36.160 --> 00:01:37.790
consumer shopping carts,
if you will, with so

00:01:37.790 --> 00:01:42.490
called radio frequency identification tags
which work like a GPS tracker, that allow

00:01:42.490 --> 00:01:47.150
us to track exactly how does somebody
walk through the store, which categories.

00:01:47.150 --> 00:01:50.486
Do they walk past,
what do they eventually end up purchasing?

00:01:50.486 --> 00:01:55.293
[MUSIC]

00:01:55.293 --> 00:01:59.912
If in a particular week the supermarket
is running a beer add do more people

00:01:59.912 --> 00:02:01.130
buy beer?

00:02:01.130 --> 00:02:03.671
That we were usually able
to observe even before, but

00:02:03.671 --> 00:02:06.726
now we can also see that actually
more people go to the beer isle.

00:02:06.726 --> 00:02:10.807
So, did that advertising attract people
to go to different part of the store that

00:02:10.807 --> 00:02:14.810
they wouldn't have otherwise gone to,
and then they made the purchase?

00:02:14.810 --> 00:02:16.140
If that's true,

00:02:16.140 --> 00:02:19.550
then maybe this advertising will have
positive spillovers to other categories.

00:02:19.550 --> 00:02:22.560
Maybe people on their way to the beer
aisle are now going to pick up

00:02:22.560 --> 00:02:24.920
salty snacks, other things.

00:02:24.920 --> 00:02:25.980
And so there's sort of, anecdotally,

00:02:25.980 --> 00:02:30.970
a lot of that sense that we are going
to get these kind of spillover effects.

00:02:30.970 --> 00:02:34.900
So people often talk about the fact that
milk gets stored in the back of the store.

00:02:34.900 --> 00:02:38.020
Why, because people buy
it very frequently, and

00:02:38.020 --> 00:02:41.570
having it in the back of the store makes
them walk past a lot of other categories.

00:02:41.570 --> 00:02:44.270
And hence, they might pick up other
things that they didn't plan on

00:02:44.270 --> 00:02:45.500
buying on their way.

00:02:46.660 --> 00:02:49.070
And so you could imagine that
advertising does something very similar.

00:02:49.070 --> 00:02:53.900
If advertising allows me to divert
traffic to other parts of the store, then

00:02:53.900 --> 00:02:58.460
maybe products I pass on that way are also
going to end up in my purchase basket.

00:02:58.460 --> 00:03:01.697
And so that was something that we
weren't usually able to observe.

00:03:01.697 --> 00:03:06.338
[MUSIC]

00:03:06.338 --> 00:03:10.589
So for a store manager, the good news here
is that advertising is quite effective

00:03:10.589 --> 00:03:13.540
beyond just the individual
product being advertised.

00:03:13.540 --> 00:03:17.450
So what we find is that, when you
advertise a particular brand of beer,

00:03:17.450 --> 00:03:19.360
sales go up in the beer
category as a whole.

00:03:19.360 --> 00:03:21.980
And that increase comes partly
from the advertised brands, but

00:03:21.980 --> 00:03:23.460
also goes beyond that.

00:03:23.460 --> 00:03:26.950
So some consumers presumably
just pick up on the fact

00:03:26.950 --> 00:03:28.710
beer is being advertised today.

00:03:28.710 --> 00:03:30.640
They get reminded of I want to buy beer,
and

00:03:30.640 --> 00:03:32.300
hence we see an increase in beer sales.

00:03:32.300 --> 00:03:35.810
And that's beyond the individual
product being advertised.

00:03:35.810 --> 00:03:39.280
At the same time we also find
that the effect stops there.

00:03:39.280 --> 00:03:43.810
So to the extent that stores might have to
hope that driving more people beer isle

00:03:43.810 --> 00:03:48.290
through advertising will then spin over
in to higher sales for salty snacks, or

00:03:48.290 --> 00:03:52.620
other categories that are stocked nearby,
we find no evidence for that whatsoever.

00:03:52.620 --> 00:03:56.300
So we think stop at category level, but

00:03:56.300 --> 00:03:59.030
they go beyond the individual
product being advertised.

00:03:59.030 --> 00:04:02.117
And so hence as we think about
allocating advertising budget,

00:04:02.117 --> 00:04:05.498
thinking about effectiveness on
the level of the product category,

00:04:05.498 --> 00:04:08.935
rather than the more narrow product
level or the broader store level,

00:04:08.935 --> 00:04:11.993
seems to be the right way to
tackle that managerial decision.

00:04:11.993 --> 00:04:16.549
[MUSIC]

